{"study_accession":"PRJNA1013316","secondary_study_accession":"SRP458944","title":"The RORɣ/SREBP2 pathway is a master regulator of cholesterol metabolism and serves as potential therapeutic target in t(4;11) leukemia","description":"Dysregulated cholesterol homeostasis promotes tumorigenesis and progression. Therefore, metabolic reprogramming constitutes a new hallmark of cancer. However, until today, only few therapeutic approaches exist to target this pathway due to the often-observed negative feedback induced by agents like statins leading to controversially increased cholesterol synthesis upon inhibition. Sterol regulatory element-binding proteins (SREBPs) are key transcription factors regulating the synthesis of cholesterol and fatty acids. Since SREBP2 is difficult to target, we performed pharmacological inhibition of retinoic acid receptor (RAR)-related orphan receptor gamma (RORγ), which acts upstream of SREBP2 and serves as master regulator of the cholesterol metabolism. This resulted in an inactivated cholesterol-related gene program with significant downregulation of cholesterol biosynthesis. Strikingly, these effects were more pronounced than the effects of fatostatin, a direct SREBP2 inhibitor. Upon RORγ inhibition, RNA sequencing showed strongly increased cholesterol efflux genes leading to leukemic cell death and cell cycle changes in a dose- and time-dependent manner. Combinatorial treatment of t(4;11) cells with the RORγ inhibitor showed additive effects with cytarabine and even strong anti-leukemia synergism with atorvastatin by circumventing the statin-induced feedback. Our results suggest a novel therapeutic strategy to inhibit tumor-specific cholesterol metabolism for the treatment of t(4;11) leukemia. Overall design: human cord blood-derived CD34+ cells were modified using CRISPR/Cas9 to carry an endogenous t(4;11) translocation. RNA sequencing was performed using TruSeq mRNA Stranded Sequencing (Illumina) from three donors in technical duplicates, comparing XY018-treated and untreated cells.","pubmed":"38030791","geo_series":"","n_samples":12,"samples":[{"sample_accession":"SAMN37286120","study_accession":"PRJNA1013316","blood_class":"cord_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"donor 3, replicate 2, XY018","description":"donor 3, replicate 2, XY018","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7762144","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-01-28","attributes":[["source_name","cord blood-derived CD34+ cells"],["tissue","cord blood-derived CD34+ cells"],["genotype","t(4;11)(q21;q23)"],["treatment","XY018"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR25918699","experiment_accession":"SRX21638460","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":8372246,"base_count":1791660644,"first_public":"2024-01-28","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM7762144: donor 3, replicate 2, XY018 Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[]},{"sample_accession":"SAMN37286121","study_accession":"PRJNA1013316","blood_class":"cord_blood","class_from":"sample","title":"donor 3, replicate 2, DMSO","description":"donor 3, replicate 2, DMSO","sex":"","geo_sample":"GSM7762143","access":"open","dbgap":"","n_runs":1,"first_public":"2024-01-28","attributes":[["source_name","cord blood-derived CD34+ cells"],["tissue","cord blood-derived CD34+ cells"],["genotype","t(4;11)(q21;q23)"],["treatment","DMSO"],["geo_loc_name","missing"],["collection_date","missing"]],"runs":[{"run_accession":"SRR25918697","experiment_accession":"SRX21638459","library_layout":"PAIRED","library_selection":"cDNA","instrument_model":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000","read_count":7676502,"base_count":1642771428,"first_public":"2024-01-28","experiment_title":"Illumina NovaSeq 6000 sequencing: GSM7762143: donor 3, replicate 2, DMSO Homo sapiens RNA-Seq"}],"labels":[]},{"sample_accession":"SAMN37286122","study_accession":"PRJNA1013316","blood_class":"cord_blood","clas